course: dictionary
lesson: 7406
title: A Christmas plant with a tricky name
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A poinsettia is a noun, which means a thing you can see and touch. The dictionary tells you it is a plant from Mexico, with large bright leaves around small yellow flowers, and that people put it up at Christmas. When you read a word you do not know, picture what it describes.
explain: The colourful parts look like petals, but they are not. Scientists call them bracts, which are special leaves. «Poinsettias are not deadly; eating them may cause a mild tummy upset» gives you context clues. The word not tells you the plant is safe, and mild tells you any upset is gentle, so you can work out that poinsettias are not very dangerous.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. If you think deadly means dangerous, then «Poinsettias are not dangerous» still makes sense with the rest of the sentence. Context clues are the words around a new word, and they often point the way to its meaning.
fact: A poinsettia is a plant from Mexico with large bright leaves and small yellow flowers.
fact: The red, pink or white parts of a poinsettia are special leaves called bracts, not petals.
fact: Poinsettias are named after Joel Poinsett, a US ambassador to Mexico.
fact: «Deadly» means able to cause death, and «mild» means gentle or not strong.
fact: «Poinsettias are not deadly» means they will not kill you, though they may upset your tummy.
exercise: Where does the poinsettia come from? | Scotland | Australia | Egypt | *Mexico
exercise: What are the red parts of a poinsettia called? | *Bracts | Petals | Roots | Seeds
exercise: «Eating them may cause a mild tummy upset.» What does mild mean here? | Long lasting | *Gentle, not strong | Very painful | Bright red
test: Who was the poinsettia named after? | *Joel Poinsett, a US ambassador to Mexico | Father Christmas | Christopher Columbus | A Scottish gardener
test: Where are the small yellow flowers on a poinsettia? | Around the edge of the pot | *In the middle of the coloured bracts | On the roots under the soil | Hanging from the ceiling
exam: Which sentence shows that eating a poinsettia is not very dangerous? | *Poinsettias are not deadly; eating them may cause a mild tummy upset | Poinsettias are named after a famous ambassador | Poinsettias are deadly, so never touch them | Poinsettias are bright red and grow in Mexico
